    {
      "topLeftX": 88,
      "topLeftY": 384,
      "bottomRightX": 397,
      "bottomRightY": 419,
      "content": "Examples: A beautiful, old, Yemeni building\na heavy, round, black stone",
      "type": "text"
    },
    {
      "topLeftX": 88,
      "topLeftY": 434,
      "bottomRightX": 170,
      "bottomRightY": 451,
      "content": "- emphasis",
      "type": "list"
    },
    {
      "topLeftX": 85,
      "topLeftY": 451,
      "bottomRightX": 606,
      "bottomRightY": 485,
      "content": "You can make your description stronger by repeating some words, for example, comparative adjectives and adverbs.",
      "type": "text"
    },
    {
      "topLeftX": 88,
      "topLeftY": 496,
      "bottomRightX": 612,
      "bottomRightY": 531,
      "content": "Examples: He drove faster and faster, and I got more and more worried.\nUp, up and up went the balloon, until it was a small dot in the sky.",
      "type": "text"
    },
    {
      "topLeftX": 87,
      "topLeftY": 543,
      "bottomRightX": 249,
      "bottomRightY": 560,
      "content": "## 2 Information focus",
      "type": "title"
    },
    {
      "topLeftX": 85,
      "topLeftY": 570,
      "bottomRightX": 619,
      "bottomRightY": 606,
      "content": "You can change the focus of a sentence by moving adjectival or adverbial phrases.\nYou may want to do this for reasons of style or emphasis.",
      "type": "text"
    },
    {
      "topLeftX": 88,
      "topLeftY": 611,
      "bottomRightX": 475,
      "bottomRightY": 647,
      "content": "Examples: There were lots of goats on top of the hill. →\nOn top of the hill were lots of goats",
      "type": "text"
    },
    {
      "topLeftX": 176,
      "topLeftY": 661,
      "bottomRightX": 500,
      "bottomRightY": 696,
      "content": "Some of the hills were to the right of the road. →\nTo the right of the road were some hills.",
      "type": "text"
    },
    {
      "topLeftX": 86,
      "topLeftY": 697,
      "bottomRightX": 528,
      "bottomRightY": 731,
      "content": "Note: In the second example the subject and the verb change places.\nThis happens only when using the verb to be.",
      "type": "text"
    },
    {
      "topLeftX": 87,
      "topLeftY": 747,
      "bottomRightX": 246,
      "bottomRightY": 767,
      "content": "## 3 Joining sentences",
      "type": "title"
    },
    {
      "topLeftX": 88,
      "topLeftY": 777,
      "bottomRightX": 232,
      "bottomRightY": 795,
      "content": "- with the -ing form",
      "type": "list"
    },
    {
      "topLeftX": 88,
      "topLeftY": 802,
      "bottomRightX": 538,
      "bottomRightY": 872,
      "content": "Examples: The children ran out of school. They were laughing and\nshouting happily. →\nThe children ran out of school, laughing and\nshouting happily.",
      "type": "text"
    },
    {
      "topLeftX": 85,
      "topLeftY": 881,
      "bottomRightX": 554,
      "bottomRightY": 914,
      "content": "Find examples of description, changed focus and joined\nsentences in the text.",
      "type": "text"
    },
    {
